      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Understanding the Role of Levels 1 to 5 in Public Administration</h2>
        <p>
          In the administrative framework of the Central Government of India, <strong>Pay Matrix Levels 1 to 5</strong> represent the largest operational segment of public employment. Spanning former Group 'C' designations across civil ministries, Indian Railways, the Department of Posts, Central Armed Police Forces (CAPFs), and Defence civilian establishments, this band encompasses millions of dedicated employees who perform vital frontline, clerical, technical, and security duties.
        </p>
        <p>
          Prior to the 7th Central Pay Commission, these designations were categorized under <strong>Pay Band 1 (PB-1: ₹5,200 to ₹20,200)</strong>, differentiated only by distinct Grade Pay amounts: GP ₹1,800, GP ₹1,900, GP ₹2,000, GP ₹2,400, and GP ₹2,800. The 7th CPC converted this structure into five autonomous horizontal functional levels, eliminating the ambiguity of grade pay overlaps.
        </p>
        <p>
          With the constitution and upcoming implementation of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong>, Levels 1 to 5 represent the primary testing ground for the Dr. Wallace Aykroyd minimum living wage formula, career stagnation safeguards, and the rationalization of junior clerical allowances.
        </p>
      </section>

