      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Vertical Increments and the 3% Compounding Mechanics</h2>
        <p>
          Every employee serving in Levels 1 through 5 is entitled to an annual increment under Fundamental Rule 26. Increments are credited on either <strong>January 1st or July 1st</strong> of each year, based on completing six months of qualifying service in that level:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The Compounding Formula:</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Next Cell Basic Pay</strong> = Current Cell Pay &plus; ( Current Cell Pay &times; 3% ), rounded off to the nearest hundred rupees.
          </p>
          <p>
            Under the Pay Matrix, employees do not calculate percentages manually. Instead, the salary automatically advances downward to the next vertical cell. For instance, an MTS employee in Level 1 moving from Cell 1 (₹41,040) to Cell 2 receives ₹42,271, gaining an immediate basic pay increase of <strong>₹1,231 per month</strong>, which also lifts their Dearness Allowance and pension contributions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Promotion and MACP Upgradation Rules (Levels 1 to 5)</h2>
        <p>
          Because promotional vacancies in Group 'C' cadres can be limited by departmental cadre strength, the <strong>Modified Assured Career Progression (MACP)</strong> scheme provides critical protection against career stagnation:
        </p>
        <ol class="editorial-list">
          <li><strong>The 10, 20, 30 Year Milestones:</strong> If an employee spends 10 years in a single post without regular departmental promotion, they are granted financial upgradation to the next functional level (e.g., Level 1 moves to Level 2; Level 2 to Level 3; Level 3 to Level 4; Level 4 to Level 5; and Level 5 to Level 6).</li>
          <li><strong>Fixation Procedure (Rule 13):</strong> 
            <ul class="sub-list">
              <li><em>Step 1:</em> The employee is granted one notional vertical increment (advancing one cell down in their existing level).</li>
              <li><em>Step 2:</em> The resulting figure is traced into the higher upgraded level. If an identical figure exists, pay is fixed in that cell. If no exact match exists, pay is placed in the <strong>immediate next higher cell</strong> of the new level.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><strong>Preserving Seniority:</strong> Financial upgradation under MACP is purely personal to the employee and does not confer functional seniority or supervisory rank, though it provides the full salary entitlement of the higher level.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

